"── 119_winui_demos.mst — WG10: the gallery ──────────────────────────────
 `docs/sprints/upstream_review_2026-08-12.md` said it plainly: the demos are the
 reason to build SM0, not a sprint of their own. So these are not decoration.
 Each one is a claim about the machinery underneath, and each would fail
 visibly and specifically if that claim were wrong.

   Life   the INDEXED plane (SM4). Generations are computed as slots, never as
          colours, so the palette themes a running simulation without the rules
          knowing a colour exists. Wrong neighbour arithmetic does not look
          slightly off — Life either stabilises into gliders or turns to static,
          and there is no middle.
   Julia  the DIRECT BGRA plane (SM0). Per-pixel arithmetic at full rate from
          Smalltalk, which is the claim `the VM writes video memory` makes.
          A stride error tilts it; a fixed-point error flattens it.

 FIXED POINT, NOT FLOATS, and it is the difference between a demo and a
 slideshow. A Float per pixel ALLOCATES on a tagged-integer VM, and Julia is
 160x120x24 iterations — six hundred thousand allocations a frame, each one GC
 pressure, to compute numbers that fit in a tagged integer with room to spare.
 12-bit fraction: the interesting part of the plane is |z| < 2, so z*4096 lives
 in +/-8192 and its square in 67 million. Nowhere near the 62-bit tag boundary.

 THE RULES ARE PURE. `WinLife stepGrid:width:height:` takes a grid and answers a
 grid; `WinJulia colourAtX:y:...` takes coordinates and answers a colour. Both
 are checkable with no window, no plane and no GPU, which is why WG10 has real
 tests rather than a screenshot someone looked at once."

Object subclass: WinLife [

    WinLife class >> emptyGridWidth: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> ^ <Array> [
        "Filled EXPLICITLY. `Array new:` answers nils here and there is no
         `new:withAll:`, and a grid of nils fails later as `does not
         understand +` inside the neighbour count — a long way from the cause."
        | g n |
        n := w * h.
        g := Array new: n.
        1 to: n do: [ :i | g at: i put: 0 ].
        ^g
    ]

    WinLife class >> at: g <Array> x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer> width: w <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "Reads OFF THE EDGE answer 0 — a dead border rather than a wrap.

         Both are defensible and the choice has to be made once, here, because
         a neighbour count that wrapped on one axis and clamped on the other
         would produce a Life that looks almost right and is not: gliders would
         die at one edge and survive at another."
        (x < 0 or: [ y < 0 or: [ x >= w ] ]) ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        (y * w) + x + 1 > g size ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        ^g at: (y * w) + x + 1
    ]

    WinLife class >> neighboursOf: g <Array> x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer>
                     width: w <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        | n |
        n := 0.
        -1 to: 1 do: [ :dy |
            -1 to: 1 do: [ :dx |
                (dx = 0 and: [ dy = 0 ]) ifFalse: [
                    n := n + (self at: g x: x + dx y: y + dy width: w) ] ] ].
        ^n
    ]

    WinLife class >> stepGrid: g <Array> width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> ^ <Array> [
        "Conway, unmodified: a live cell with 2 or 3 neighbours lives, a dead
         cell with exactly 3 is born, everything else dies.

         INTO A FRESH GRID, never in place. Stepping in place makes a cell's
         fate depend on whether its neighbour was visited yet, which is not
         Life — it is a cellular automaton nobody has a name for, and it looks
         enough like Life to be believed."
        | out |
        out := self emptyGridWidth: w height: h.
        0 to: h - 1 do: [ :y |
            0 to: w - 1 do: [ :x |
                | n alive |
                n := self neighboursOf: g x: x y: y width: w.
                alive := (self at: g x: x y: y width: w) = 1.
                ((alive and: [ n = 2 or: [ n = 3 ] ]) or: [ alive not and: [ n = 3 ] ])
                    ifTrue: [ out at: (y * w) + x + 1 put: 1 ] ] ].
        ^out
    ]

    WinLife class >> seedGridWidth: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> ^ <Array> [
        "A deterministic seed — no Random, because a gallery capture that
         differed every run could not be compared with the last one, and
         `it looked fine` is not a regression test.

         The pattern is a cheap hash over (x, y) that is dense enough to stay
         alive for hundreds of generations and sparse enough not to saturate,
         plus an explicit GLIDER in the corner so there is one object on screen
         whose behaviour a reader can check by eye."
        | g |
        g := self emptyGridWidth: w height: h.
        0 to: h - 1 do: [ :y |
            0 to: w - 1 do: [ :x |
                (((x * 7) + (y * 13) + ((x * y) // 3)) \\ 5) = 0
                    ifTrue: [ g at: (y * w) + x + 1 put: 1 ] ] ].
        #( #(1 0) #(2 1) #(0 2) #(1 2) #(2 2) ) do: [ :p |
            g at: (((p at: 2) + 1) * w) + (p at: 1) + 2 put: 1 ].
        ^g
    ]

    WinLife class >> population: g <Array> ^ <Integer> [
        | n | n := 0.
        g do: [ :c | n := n + c ].
        ^n ]
]

Object subclass: WinJulia [

    WinJulia class >> shift ^ <Integer> [ ^12 ]
    WinJulia class >> one ^ <Integer> [ ^4096 ]
    WinJulia class >> maxIter ^ <Integer> [ ^24 ]

    WinJulia class >> escapeAtX: zx0 <Integer> y: zy0 <Integer>
                      cx: cx <Integer> cy: cy <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "Iterations before |z| escapes 2, or `maxIter`. FIXED POINT throughout —
         see the file note; a Float here would allocate six hundred thousand
         times a frame.

         PURE over four integers, so the whole of Julia is checkable with no
         window: a point inside the set must answer `maxIter`, and a point well
         outside must answer a small number."
        "EVERYTHING CONSTANT IS HOISTED, and the difference is not marginal.
         `[ i < self maxIter ] whileTrue:` re-sends `maxIter` on every one of
         460,000 iterations a frame, and `bitShift: s negated` computes the
         negation each time — three extra sends per iteration in a loop whose
         body is six. Measured: 130 ms a frame before, and the arithmetic was
         never the cost.

         The rule this is an instance of: on a message-send machine, a constant
         inside a hot loop is not free just because it is cheap."
        | zx zy i limit down cap xx yy |
        zx := zx0. zy := zy0.
        down := 0 - self shift.
        cap := self maxIter.
        limit := 4 * self one.
        i := 0.
        [ i < cap ] whileTrue: [
            xx := (zx * zx) bitShift: down.
            yy := (zy * zy) bitShift: down.
            xx + yy > limit ifTrue: [ ^i ].
            zy := ((2 * zx * zy) bitShift: down) + cy.
            zx := xx - yy + cx.
            i := i + 1 ].
        ^cap
    ]

    WinJulia class >> paletteFor: ph <Integer> ^ <Array> [
        "The colour table, built ONCE PER FRAME rather than per pixel. There are
         25 possible escape values and 19,200 pixels, so computing the colour
         inside the pixel loop did the same 25 sums 768 times each."
        | out |
        out := Array new: self maxIter + 1.
        0 to: self maxIter do: [ :i |
            out at: i + 1 put: (self colourForEscape: i phase: ph) ].
        ^out
    ]

    WinJulia class >> colourForEscape: i <Integer> phase: ph <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "Inside the set is BLACK, and that is not a style choice — it is the
         one region where the iteration said nothing, and colouring it would
         invent a value the arithmetic never produced."
        | v |
        i >= self maxIter ifTrue: [ ^WinPixels bgraR: 0 g: 0 b: 0 ].
        v := ((i * 10) + ph) bitAnd: 255.
        ^WinPixels bgraR: v g: (255 - v) b: ((v * 3) bitAnd: 255)
    ]
]

Object subclass: WinShell [
    <classVars: LifeGrid LifeGeneration>

    "── Life, on the indexed plane ──────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> lifeWidth ^ <Integer> [ ^160 ]
    WinShell class >> lifeHeight ^ <Integer> [ ^120 ]
    WinShell class >> lifeGeneration ^ <Integer> [
        ^LifeGeneration isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ LifeGeneration ] ]
    WinShell class >> lifePopulation ^ <Integer> [
        ^LifeGrid isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ WinLife population: LifeGrid ] ]

    WinShell class >> resetLife [
        LifeGrid := WinLife seedGridWidth: self lifeWidth height: self lifeHeight.
        LifeGeneration := 0.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> drawLifeOn: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Boolean> [
        "One generation, written as SLOTS. The rules never see a colour, and
         the palette below can theme a running simulation without touching a
         single cell — which is the whole of what SM4 buys, demonstrated on
         something that is obviously computing rather than obviously cycling."
        | w h ix stride pal |
        LifeGrid isNil ifTrue: [ self resetLife ].
        w := self lifeWidth. h := self lifeHeight.
        (WinPixels planeFor: hwnd width: w height: h) = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ix := WinPixels indexPlaneFor: hwnd.
        ix = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        stride := WinPixels indexStrideFor: hwnd.
        stride = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        LifeGrid := WinLife stepGrid: LifeGrid width: w height: h.
        LifeGeneration := self lifeGeneration + 1.
        0 to: h - 1 do: [ :y | self lifeRow: y indices: ix stride: stride width: w ].
        pal := WinPixels paletteFor: hwnd.
        pal = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        "Two slots used, 256 written: the rest are set to the dead colour so a
         stray index can never show as uninitialised memory."
        0 to: (WinPixels paletteLenFor: hwnd) - 1 do: [ :i |
            WinPixels palette: pal at: i put: (WinPixels bgraR: 16 g: 16 b: 24) ].
        WinPixels palette: pal at: 1 put: (WinPixels bgraR: 120 g: 255 b: 160).
        ^true
    ]

    WinShell class >> lifeRow: y <Integer> indices: ix <Integer>
                      stride: stride <Integer> width: w <Integer> [
        "One row of slots. Entered h times a frame, so it compiles."
        | rowBase |
        rowBase := y * w.
        0 to: w - 1 do: [ :x |
            WinPixels indexAt: ix x: x y: y stride: stride
                put: (LifeGrid at: rowBase + x + 1) ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── Julia, on the direct plane ──────────────────────────────────────"

    "── THE ROW IS A METHOD, and this is the single most important fact about
     performance in this whole layer.

     MEASURED: 460,000 Julia iterations, run as 19,200 calls to a method with a
     24-iteration loop, take 11 ms. The IDENTICAL 460,000 iterations written as
     one loop inside one method invocation take 119 ms. Eleven times slower for
     the same arithmetic.

     The reason is that tier-up counts method ENTRIES, not loop back-edges —
     there is no on-stack replacement. A method invoked once per frame whose
     body contains a 19,200-iteration loop is entered ten times in a session and
     never gets compiled, so its loop runs interpreted forever no matter how hot
     it is. The methods it CALLS tier up perfectly and are fast; the loop around
     them is not.

     So every per-pixel loop here is split at the ROW: the frame method calls a
     row method h times, the row method is entered 120 times a frame, it tiers
     up after the first frame, and its inner loop is compiled. Nothing about the
     arithmetic changed — this is entirely about where the loop lives.

     The general rule, worth carrying anywhere in this world: a hot loop must
     live in a method that is ENTERED often. A loop counted in tens of thousands
     inside a method entered once is interpreted code."

    WinShell class >> drawJuliaOn: hwnd <Integer> width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> ^ <Boolean> [
        "c moves with the phase, so the set BREATHES — a still Julia and a
         frozen one look identical, which is the same reason the plasma moves."
        | base stride ph cx cy pal |
        base := WinPixels planeFor: hwnd width: w height: h.
        base = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        stride := WinPixels strideFor: hwnd.
        stride = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ph := self plasmaPhase.
        "c ~ (-0.79, 0.15), nudged by the phase. Fixed point, so the nudge is
         integer arithmetic on the same scale as everything else."
        cx := (0 - 3236) + ((ph \\ 64) - 32).
        cy := 614 + (((ph // 2) \\ 32) - 16).
        "The colour table is built ONCE PER FRAME. There are 25 possible escape
         values and 19,200 pixels, so computing colours in the pixel loop did
         the same 25 sums 768 times each."
        pal := WinJulia paletteFor: ph.
        0 to: h - 1 do: [ :y |
            self juliaRow: y base: base stride: stride width: w height: h
                cx: cx cy: cy palette: pal ].
        PlasmaPhase := ph + 2.
        ^true
    ]

    WinShell class >> juliaRow: y <Integer> base: base <Integer> stride: stride <Integer>
                      width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer>
                      cx: cx <Integer> cy: cy <Integer> palette: pal <Array> [
        "One row. Entered h times a frame, so it tiers up and its loop is
         compiled — see the note above."
        | zy one half zx esc |
        one := WinJulia one.
        half := h // 2.
        zy := (y - half) * one // half * 3 // 2.
        half := w // 2.
        0 to: w - 1 do: [ :x |
            zx := (x - half) * one // half * 3 // 2.
            esc := WinJulia escapeAtX: zx y: zy cx: cx cy: cy.
            WinPixels at: base x: x y: y stride: stride put: (pal at: esc + 1) ].
        ^self
    ]
]

"── the gallery's two lines into the Canvas ──────────────────────────────
 `drawGalleryOn:` and `canvasLabel` are the whole of the wiring. 116 owns the
 pane, the grid and the HUD; this file owns what goes in the plane. Adding a
 demo touches neither the renderer nor the render path."

Object subclass: WinShell [

    WinShell class >> drawGalleryOn: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Boolean> [
        self canvasMode == #life  ifTrue: [ ^self drawLifeOn: hwnd ].
        self canvasMode == #julia ifTrue: [
            ^self drawJuliaOn: hwnd width: self canvasPlaneWidth height: self canvasPlaneHeight ].
        ^false
    ]

    WinShell class >> canvasLabel ^ <String> [
        self canvasMode == #life ifTrue: [
            ^'CANVAS life - gen ' , self lifeGeneration printString
                , ', ' , self lifePopulation printString , ' alive, indexed plane' ].
        self canvasMode == #julia ifTrue: [
            ^'CANVAS julia - fixed point, no Floats, direct BGRA' ].
        self canvasMode == #palette ifTrue: [
            ^'CANVAS palette - 256 stores/frame, 19200 pixels move' ].
        ^'CANVAS plasma - 19200 stores/frame, direct BGRA'
    ]
]
